.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt SOCAT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm socat ,
.Nm filan ,
.Nm procan
.Nd relay bytes between two streams, and list a process's descriptors and parameters
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm socat
.Op Ar options
.Ar address1
.Ar address2
.Nm filan
.Op Ar options
.Nm procan
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm socat
opens the two addresses and copies the bytes each gives to the other until
both have ended.
An address is a type and its parameters, then options after commas: among
them
.Ql TCP: Ns Ar host : Ns Ar port ,
.Ql TCP-LISTEN: Ns Ar port ,
.Ql UDP: Ns Ar host : Ns Ar port ,
.Ql UNIX-CONNECT: Ns Ar path ,
.Ql OPENSSL: Ns Ar host : Ns Ar port ,
.Ql EXEC: Ns Ar command ,
.Ql FILE: Ns Ar path ,
and
.Ql -
for standard input and output.
.Ql socat -h
lists the address types and their options.
.Pp
.Nm filan
prints each open descriptor of its own process with what it refers to: the
type of file, its device and inode, its flags and, for a socket, its family
and addresses.
.Nm procan
prints the parameters of its own process: its ids, its terminal, its
resource limits and the values
.Fn sysconf
gives.
.Pp
The GNU root has the three as
.Pa /usr/bin/socat ,
.Pa /usr/bin/filan
and
.Pa /usr/bin/procan ,
from the socat 1.8.1.3 port with OpenSSL for the TLS addresses, and with
the release's scripts
.Pa socat-broker.sh ,
.Pa socat-chain.sh
and
.Pa socat-mux.sh
beside them.
Claude Code's sandbox runs
.Nm socat
to join its proxy to the loopback interface of the sandbox's network
namespace, as
.Xr claude 1
says.
On the GNU root
.Ql man socat
shows the release's own page, which describes every address and option.
.Ss Rach2's limits
.Bl -bullet
.It
There is no
.Ql READLINE
address: socat is under the GPL version 2 alone and Readline under
version 3, so the port leaves it out, as Debian does.
.It
The SCTP, DCCP, VSOCK and TUN addresses fail: the kernel has none of
those sockets and no
.Pa /dev/net/tun .
.It
A raw IPv4 socket carries ICMP alone, and a raw IPv6 socket ICMPv6 or the
datagrams the caller builds whole, so
.Ql IP-SENDTO ,
.Ql IP4-RECV
and the other raw addresses work only with those protocols, and as root.
A packet socket is
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
alone, so
.Ql INTERFACE
fails.
.It
A program can join no IPv4 multicast group:
.Ql ip-add-membership
is accepted, and the socket receives no datagram sent to the group.
.It
TCP segments leave with hop limit 64 and class 0, whatever the
.Ql ip-ttl ,
.Ql ipv6-unicast-hops
and
.Ql ipv6-tclass
options set.
.It
A network namespace other than the first has IPv4 alone on its loopback
interface, so in Claude Code's sandbox
.Nm socat
listens and connects on 127.0.0.1 and not on
.Ql ::1 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm socat
exits 0 when both streams ended or a timeout ended the relay, and 1 on an
error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Serve one connection on port 7777 by echoing what it sends:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ socat TCP-LISTEN:7777,reuseaddr EXEC:cat
.Ed
.Pp
Talk to a TLS server from the terminal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ socat - OPENSSL:example.org:443
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr claude 1 ,
.Xr nc 1 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr ipv6 7 ,
.Xr tcp 7 ,
.Xr unix 7
.Pp
.Lk http://www.dest-unreach.org/socat/ "socat"
